    {
      "a": "leonardo",
      "b": "michelangelo",
      "kind": "rivals in Florence, 1504",
      "word": "Florence",
      "note": "In 1504 Florence commissioned battle scenes for the same room of the Palazzo Vecchio from both of them at once. Leonardo was fifty-two and famous; Michelangelo was twenty-nine and had just finished the David. Neither fresco was finished, and the cartoons — copied by every young painter in Italy, Raphael among them — became the most influential drawings of the century."
    },
    {
      "a": "raphael",
      "b": "leonardo",
      "kind": "learned sfumato from",
      "word": "Leonardo",
      "note": "Raphael reached Florence around 1504 and rebuilt his art on what he found there. The pyramid composition of his Madonnas and the soft smoke-edged modelling of his faces are Leonardo’s, absorbed in about two years by a painter thirty years younger."
    },
    {
      "a": "raphael",
      "b": "michelangelo",
      "kind": "worked down the corridor from",
      "word": "Michelangelo",
      "note": "From 1508 they worked a few hundred metres apart in the same palace — Michelangelo on the Sistine ceiling, Raphael on the Stanze — and did not get on. Raphael saw the half-finished ceiling and promptly painted Michelangelo into the School of Athens as brooding Heraclitus, sitting alone on the steps."
    },
    {
      "a": "michelangelo",
      "b": "giotto",
      "kind": "drew after",
      "word": "Giotto",
      "note": "Michelangelo’s earliest surviving drawings are copies after Giotto’s frescoes in Santa Croce. Two centuries separate them; the weight and solidity of the bodies passed straight across."
    },
    {
      "a": "caravaggio",
      "b": "rembrandt",
      "kind": "the light that reached",
      "word": "light",
      "note": "Caravaggio never went north, but his followers did. By the 1620s the Utrecht painters had carried the single raking light and the hard black shadow back to Holland, where the young Rembrandt took it and made it his own."
    },
    {
      "a": "veronese",
      "b": "el-greco",
      "kind": "Venice-trained alongside",
      "word": "Venice",
      "note": "Both learned to paint in Venice in the shadow of Titian — Veronese from Verona, El Greco from Crete — and both took the Venetian habit of building a figure out of colour rather than drawing it first."
    },
    {
      "a": "velazquez",
      "b": "manet",
      "kind": "\"the painter of painters\", to",
      "word": "Velázquez",
      "note": "Manet went to Madrid in 1865 and stood in front of the Velázquezes. “The painter of painters,” he wrote home. The flat black, the refusal to model with shadow and the frank grey ground of Olympia all came back from that trip."
