    {
      "a": "velazquez",
      "b": "sargent",
      "kind": "copied in Madrid by",
      "word": "Velázquez",
      "note": "Sargent copied Velázquez in the Prado at twenty-three. Madame X’s silhouette against a bare ground — the whole figure carried by one contour — is what he took away from it."
    },
    {
      "a": "david",
      "b": "ingres",
      "kind": "taught",
      "word": "David",
      "note": "Ingres entered David’s studio at seventeen and stayed four years. He inherited the line and abandoned the politics, and spent his life being called the last classicist by people who could not see how strange his drawing had become."
    },
    {
      "a": "gericault",
      "b": "delacroix",
      "kind": "who posed for the Raft",
      "word": "Géricault",
      "note": "They were friends, and Delacroix posed for the Raft of the Medusa — he is the figure lying face down with one arm outstretched in the foreground. Géricault died at thirty-two; Delacroix carried Romanticism for another forty years."
    },
    {
