      "b": "vangogh",
      "kind": "copied, again and again, by",
      "word": "Millet",
      "note": "Van Gogh copied Millet all his life, from the drawings he made as a beginner to the canvases he painted in the asylum at Saint-Rémy. “Millet is father Millet,” he wrote, “counsellor and mentor in everything.”"
    },
    {
      "a": "manet",
      "b": "monet",
      "kind": "friends, and forever confused",
      "word": "Manet",
      "note": "The two names were confused from the start — Manet complained when a Monet was praised as his at the Salon of 1865 — and they became friends anyway. Manet painted Monet at work in his floating studio at Argenteuil."
    },
    {
      "a": "manet",
      "b": "degas",
      "kind": "friends and antagonists",
      "word": "Degas",
      "note": "Friends, and combative about it. Degas gave Manet a portrait of Manet and his wife; Manet, disliking how his wife had been painted, cut her out of it with a knife. Degas took the picture back, and they did not speak for a while."
    },
    {
      "a": "manet",
      "b": "cezanne",
      "kind": "answered by a Modern Olympia",
      "word": "Olympia",
      "note": "Cézanne answered Olympia in 1873 with A Modern Olympia — the same scene painted as a violent sketch, with himself sitting in the picture watching. It hung in the first Impressionist exhibition and was the most mocked object there."
    },
    {
      "a": "monet",
      "b": "renoir",
      "kind": "painted side by side at La Grenouillère",
      "word": "Renoir",
      "note": "In the summer of 1869 they set up their easels side by side at La Grenouillère on the Seine and painted the same bathers, the same boats, the same broken light on the water. Impressionism is roughly what they worked out that summer."
    },
    {
      "a": "caillebotte",
      "b": "renoir",
      "kind": "collected and left to France",
      "word": "Caillebotte",
      "note": "Caillebotte painted with them and, being rich, bought from them. His will left the collection to the French state, and Renoir was his executor."
